<p>Three more of John's poems — "A Harmless Loon," the paired "Where Danger Lurks" and "Needle Teeth," and "Fare" — bookending two personal stories he frames as matched temperature extremes: the January 1994 morning he went under the ice on Cebolla Creek breaking water for cattle, and a blistering August 1981 afternoon prospecting in South Dakota, between Spearfish and Lead, that finally gave him his turn at a waterfall he'd been eyeing for weeks.</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>A Harmless Loon</h2>

  <p>She sat me down with measured frown<br>
  to pet my full attention<br>
  her brows were knit, her pupils lit<br>
  with flames of ill contention</p>

  <p>A troubled sigh was followed by<br>
  a question of my worth<br>
  and how to train my feeble brain<br>
  unused since time of birth</p>

  <p>My faults are few but how they grew<br>
  when teamed with female wiles<br>
  that probe the id and soon get rid<br>
  of unresponsive files</p>

  <p>Wherein my life has dealt with strife<br>
  performing a charade<br>
  that spells buffoon, a harmless loon<br>
  who failed to make the grade</p>

  <p>Well hell, my dear, you've naught to fear<br>
  I rarely show my face<br>
  or flaunt my name to cause you shame<br>
  and bring unjust disgrace</p>

  <p>Please trust in me, I'm sure you'll see<br>
  I've finally come to grips<br>
  with slothful ways that mar your days<br>
  as brandy nears my lips</p>

  <p>Why just tonight I feel so bright<br>
  my thinking is much quicker<br>
  we must construe that this is due<br>
  to spirits in hard liquor</p>

  <p class="source-doc-signature">Thundermug 2002</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>Where Danger Lurks</h2>

  <p>Two creatures I won't tangle with<br>
  in slumber or awake<br>
  a miner with a hangover<br>
  or a sunburned rattlesnake</p>

  <h2>Needle Teeth</h2>

  <p>One must respect the weasel<br>
  and grant it a wide berth<br>
  a weasel kills in sheer delight<br>
  and fears nothing on this Earth</p>

  <p class="source-doc-signature">Thundermug 2002</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>The Coldest Day</h2>

  <p>The coldest short period my body has been exposed to was on Cebolla Creek in January of 1994. We had fifty head of Cadwell ranch cattle on upper facilities of our ranch and keeping water holes open for them was an early morning chore I had solved by making small, temporary dams with ice that I barred loose on a bend of the creek with slow moving water.</p>

  <p>It was approaching 20° below zero with an ever present wind on this day as I took bar and shovel to the ice with cattle strung out waiting for their first drink of the day. It went well in spite of the cold and I had only one block to maneuver to partially check the stream and raise the level of the water to where three or four cows could drink at a time.</p>

  <p>The ice was slick from a new snow and I was on my hands and knees with the shovel pushing the one remaining block into position. The block rolled, the shovel skated over it and I lost my balance. I seemed to roll rather slowly but was unable to stop and landed in the creek face up under water. I had held on to the shovel, luckily, and was able to use it to right myself. I was scrambling and pushing with the shovel handle to get back on solid ice when a neighbor, John Martin, from a ranch to the southwest, drove by on his way to feed cattle. He waved and went on.</p>

  <p>There was no need to panic once on solid footing and the house and warmth were only a quarter mile away. I wasn't hurt and snow depth was minimal but I had two barbed wire fences to negotiate in a beeline. I was able to straddle the first one and throw my left leg over but by the time I reached the second fence my clothes were frozen so solid all I could do was push my midsection against the top wire and roll over, dropping to the other side. I reached the house, hollered for <a href="/people/john-richard-jackson/#inez-mae-schriver">Inez</a> to open the door, and I shuffled to the wood heater as she gasped and ran for a rug to place by the heater to soak up the water. I was completely ice crusted from top to bottom, the lashes of my left eye were frozen shut and my right eye was barely open.</p>

  <p>Several days later I met John Martin and asked him why he hadn't helped me. He replied, "Hell, I didn't know what you were doing down there. I went home and told Bobbie the biggest sucker I had ever seen was floundering around in the creek by Jackson's meadow."</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>Fare</h2>

  <p>Lorda 'mighty, boys, I'm cold<br>
  I think my feet are froze<br>
  what good is all this gawdam gold<br>
  if ice is all it grows</p>

  <p>Let's hit that riffle one more time<br>
  the colors still look good<br>
  before the bottom starts to slime<br>
  from all that mossy wood</p>

  <p>What in hell possesses man<br>
  to rummage down in gravel<br>
  for specks of gold to coat his pan<br>
  and make his brain unravel</p>

  <p>A night or two with a drunken whore<br>
  and a rousing poker game<br>
  have taken all my dust and more<br>
  I love 'em just the same</p>

  <p>For life is life to live as well<br>
  as rugged men see fit<br>
  to buck the odds and live to tell<br>
  the meaning of "true grit"</p>

  <p>Let's clean the sluice and bar the ditch<br>
  We'll get our gear together<br>
  and leave this freezin' sonuvabitch<br>
  'cause, boys, it looks like weather</p>

  <p class="source-doc-signature">Thundermug 2003</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2>The Hottest Day</h2>

  <p>The warmest lengthy period my body has been exposed to was in August 1981 between the towns of Spearfish and Lead, South Dakota while working in and near Squaw Canyon. Anne Dutka, geologist in temporary status, and I were running grid lines and taking soil samples for Freeport Exploration Company on a gold prospect.</p>

  <p>Previously, and in cooler temperatures, while running a grid, we had passed a waterfall where two young men and two young women were frolicking under the water completely nude. I had been anxious to get back after that but we had been delayed in another area. Now it was August, blistering hot and we had been exposed to the sun all morning on a westerly slope. I was in meltdown, my clothes were plastered to me, my boots were squishing with perspiration, and I had taken the bulk of the samples to ease Anne's burden. She was a very willing and energetic partner and never complained, however it was apparent she was suffering from the heat as much as I.</p>

  <p>We had passed an abandoned mine, overgrown with brush and just begging for some exploring. I made note of it on a topo map hoping to return when it was cooler and I had more time. That line took us by the waterfall where we had seen the nudies and I was determined to join any and all in such comfort to reduce my body temperature, not to raise it.</p>

  <p>The waterfall was just a trickle. The spring that supplied it was nearly dry but less than a hundred feet from the overhang a hole had been eroded or sculpted in some manner to form a basin four feet deep by perhaps eight feet in length wide, enough to fit my frame into. I told Anne she could go hide herself or join me, that I was going to undress and wade in. The hole was in bedrock and clean. Anne declined to join and disappeared.</p>

  <p>I hung my clothes on nearby bushes and eased into the most refreshing bath I have ever had. I took only a few minutes but it was so hot my clothes were nearly dried, including my socks which had been dripping when I took them off. On the return, up a hill to the east, I stopped to catch my breath, stooped over with my hands on my knees to help take a little of the weight off the pack from my lower back, and a snake, at least three feet long, slithered between my feet and coiled up in the shade of a pine tree. He was too hot and tired to strike, I was too tired to care.</p>
</section>
